{
  "configuration_id": "config_050",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_001 (multimodal, pca_n2000, imagenet_vit_l, avgpool, concat) achieved 62.9% acc, while image-only config_009 (imagenet_vit_l) reached only 33.2%. This ~30-point gap shows RNA is the dominant modality, making the RNA encoder the highest-leverage design choice. Question: Does upgrading the RNA encoder from a linear PCA projection (pca_n2000) to a learned, context-aware transformer (scgpt) improve accuracy while keeping all other settings identical to the current best? config_050 (scgpt, imagenet_vit_l, avgpool, concat) isolates exactly this single variable, providing a clean comparison against config_001. Why this is the best next experiment: With 7 experiments remaining, establishing whether a learned RNA encoder helps (or hurts) is the most uncertainty-reducing step. If scgpt improves accuracy, subsequent experiments can explore scgpt combined with transformer aggregation or weighted fusion. If it does not, we confirm PCA suffices and pivot to aggregation/fusion changes. Expected cost: scgpt is a pretrained transformer encoder (heavier than PCA), so I estimate ~5.5–6.5 GPU hours, modestly more expensive than config_001's 4.54 hrs but well within remaining budget."
}

